WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.093
Today we revise Chapter Two.

00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:04.784
Data organisation, and data control.

00:00:04.784 --> 00:00:09.792
How to reduce input errors, and how data is organised and accessed.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.073
Remember four threads.

00:00:12.073 --> 00:00:15.058
G I G O — guard against bad input.

00:00:15.058 --> 00:00:17.780
Validation versus verification.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.974
Common validation checks.

00:00:19.974 --> 00:00:23.486
Data hierarchy, and file access methods.

00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:26.097
Exam warning: stop G I G O.

00:00:26.097 --> 00:00:28.107
Garbage-In, Garbage-Out.

00:00:28.107 --> 00:00:31.876
Useless input always produces useless output.

00:00:32.226 --> 00:00:36.045
Manual entry always risks human error — three main types.

00:00:36.045 --> 00:00:40.400
First, data source error: the source person fills in wrong facts.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.228
Second, transcription error: misread or mistype — for example one and L, or zero and O.

00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:52.794
Third, transposition error: neighbouring characters swap — for example sixteen typed as sixty-one.

00:00:53.144 --> 00:00:55.778
Both fight errors, but do not confuse them.

00:00:55.778 --> 00:01:00.372
Validation checks whether data is reasonable, before the system accepts it.

00:01:00.372 --> 00:01:06.008
Verification checks whether keyed data matches the original source, after entry is finished.

00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:10.177
Validation happens before data is received or processed.

00:01:10.177 --> 00:01:14.746
Its aim: is the input reasonable, logical, and within preset rules?

00:01:14.746 --> 00:01:17.951
Its limit: legal but false data can still pass.

00:01:17.951 --> 00:01:21.838
A fake phone number in a valid format still gets through.

00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:25.476
Verification happens after data entry is complete.

00:01:25.476 --> 00:01:30.540
Its aim: confirm the keyed data matches the original source document exactly.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.790
Two common methods.

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:36.328
Input data twice — for example type a new password twice and compare.

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:42.444
Double data entry: two operators key the same data independently, then the computer compares.

00:01:42.794 --> 00:01:45.688
The following are validation-check examples.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:47.333
Remember: parity checking

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:49.899
detects transmission or storage errors.

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:53.714
It is not a validation check, and it is not a check digit.

00:01:54.064 --> 00:01:57.144
Presence check: a required field must not be empty.

00:01:57.144 --> 00:02:03.665
Field length check: limit character or digit length — a Hong Kong phone number must be exactly eight digits.

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:11.032
Range check: a value must lie between a set lower and upper bound — scores zero to one hundred; birth month one to twelve.

00:02:11.382 --> 00:02:16.936
Fixed value check: data must belong to a predefined list — gender only male or female.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:22.102
Format check: the pattern must follow a rule — an email must contain an at sign.

00:02:22.102 --> 00:02:26.622
Type check: the data type must be correct — age accepts integers only.

00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:30.714
A check digit uses a maths algorithm, often with weights,

00:02:30.714 --> 00:02:34.390
to compute a trailing check character for self-checking.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.511
Examples: the digit in brackets on a Hong Kong ID card, I S B N, and barcodes.

00:02:39.511 --> 00:02:42.596
Remember: this does not include the parity bit.

00:02:42.946 --> 00:02:46.048
E D B data hierarchy, from small to large.

00:02:46.048 --> 00:02:49.223
Data: the underlying binary bits and bytes.

00:02:49.223 --> 00:02:52.398
A field: the smallest unit a user accesses.

00:02:52.398 --> 00:02:56.386
A record: the related fields that describe one entity.

00:02:56.386 --> 00:03:01.186
A file, or table: a set of records that share the same structure.

00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:05.026
A database: a collection of related files or tables.

00:03:05.376 --> 00:03:08.465
E D B lists only two file-access methods.

00:03:08.465 --> 00:03:10.349
First, sequential access.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:12.610
Typical medium: magnetic tape.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:18.638
You must read from start to end in order; seek time is long and hard to predict.

00:03:18.638 --> 00:03:22.104
Common for batch processing or offline backup.

00:03:22.454 --> 00:03:23.909
Second, direct access.

00:03:23.909 --> 00:03:27.743
Typical media: hard disk, optical disc, solid-state drive.

00:03:27.743 --> 00:03:31.115
You can locate a record directly by address or key.

00:03:31.115 --> 00:03:35.744
Seek time is shorter and more predictable; good for real-time queries.

00:03:35.744 --> 00:03:41.562
At the course file-access level, so-called random access still counts as direct access —

00:03:41.562 --> 00:03:46.190
do not confuse that with device-level random versus sequential traits.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:47.945
Remember four points.

00:03:47.945 --> 00:03:52.494
G I G O: garbage in, garbage out — know the three human-error types.

00:03:52.494 --> 00:03:58.380
Validation checks reasonableness; verification matches the source — do not confuse them.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:03.464
Know the seven common validation checks; parity checking is not one of them.

00:04:03.464 --> 00:04:08.548
Five levels in the data hierarchy; file access is only sequential or direct.
